{
  "esm": "1.1.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "NTiedtkeColumnInitialization",
    "description": "`cuinin`, the routine that builds the grid New Tiedtke's mass-flux scheme actually works on (`phys/physics_mmm/cu_ntiedtke.F90:1091-1216`, WRF 4.8). Everything above it in the scheme -- the trigger, the updraught, the downdraught, the fluxes -- reads HALF-LEVEL environmental values, and this is where they come from.\n\nThree things happen, and only the first is interpolation.\n\n1. HALF-LEVEL VALUES BY DRY STATIC ENERGY, not by temperature. The half-level temperature is taken from the LARGER of the two adjacent layers' dry static energies, s = cp T + phi, and converted back at the interface geopotential: T_h = (max(s[k-1], s[k]) - phi_h)/cp. Interpolating s rather than T is what makes the half-level value conserve the quantity a vertical displacement conserves; taking the maximum is the first half of the static-stability adjustment. The half-level humidity starts as the UPPER layer's value, and the half-level saturation value likewise.\n\n2. THE HALF-LEVEL PAIR IS PUT ON ITS SATURATION CURVE, by the kcall = 0 mode of `cuadjtqn` -- the same two Newton steps `saturation_adjustment.esm` pins, applied here over the column's own axis through the shared templates rather than restated. The humidity is then reconstructed as min(q, q_sat) of the upper layer plus whatever the adjustment moved q_sat by, floored at zero: the half level inherits the layer's SUBSATURATION, not its mixing ratio. The adjustment runs only over 2 <= k <= NLEV-2; the Fortran's `if (jk >= klev-1 .or. jk < 2) cycle` skips it at the two lowest half levels AND skips the humidity reconstruction with it, so those keep the raw upper-layer value.\n\n3. THE COLUMN IS MADE STATICALLY STABLE, by a running maximum of the half-level dry static energy taken from the SURFACE UPWARD. The Fortran writes it as a downward sweep, `s[jk] <- max(s[jk], s[jk+1])` for jk = klev-1 .. 2, which reads the cell it wrote on the previous iteration and so looks like a recurrence. It is not one: unrolling it gives s[k] = max over j >= k of the ORIGINAL s[j], whose terms do not depend on the result, so it is a reverse prefix reduction (esm-spec 4.3.1.1 reserves recurrences for folds whose next term is a function of the previous ANSWER, which this is not). It is written here as that reduction. `max` is exactly associative, so the reverse-scan reassociation caveat of esm-spec 4.3.1 does not apply.\n\nThe level of strongest ascent, `klwmin`, is likewise a scan in the Fortran and an argmin here: the level in [3, NLEV] where omega is most negative, and the SURFACE level when the column is nowhere ascending. Because the scan updates only on a STRICT improvement and runs from the surface upward, a tie is kept by the LOWEST of the tied levels, which is what the aggregate below reproduces.\n\nVERTICAL ORDERING IS THE SCHEME'S, TOP-DOWN: index 1 is the model top and index NLEV the surface, the ordering `cu_ntiedtke_pre_run` builds and every routine inside the scheme uses. The components of this scheme keep it, and the reversal back to WRF's bottom-up ordering happens once, at the tendency stage, exactly as `cu_ntiedtke_post_run` does it. No continuous vertical operator appears here, so no grid is imported; the stages that do have one will supply a monotone coordinate in this same ordering, which pressure is.\n\nTWO PLACES WHERE THE FORTRAN LEAVES SOMETHING UNDEFINED, both reproduced rather than tidied. `pqsenh(:,1)` is never assigned by `cuinin` although the array is `intent(out)`, so its top-level entry is whatever memory held; this component sets it to the top layer's saturation value, the natural extension of how the top entries of T_h and q_h are set, and the tests mask that one entry rather than pretend to match uninitialised memory. And `cumastrn` declares `pqsen` as `dimension(klon)` while every routine it hands it to declares it `dimension(klon,klev)` -- legal by Fortran sequence association, and harmless only because `cumastrn` never indexes it itself (FORTRAN_BUGS N110).",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "wrf",
      "convection",
      "new-tiedtke",
      "half-levels",
      "static-stability",
      "column"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Tiedtke, M. (1989). A comprehensive mass flux scheme for cumulus parameterization in large-scale models. Mon. Wea. Rev. 117, 1779-1800.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1989)117<1779:ACMFSF>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR/MMM-physics cu_ntiedtke.F90 (WRF 4.8 phys/physics_mmm submodule), subroutine cuinin."
      }
    ]
  },
  "metaparameters": {
    "NLEV": {
      "type": "integer",
      "default": 59,
      "description": "Number of model layers in the scheme's own top-down ordering (the kernel's klev); 59 is the chem170 single-column reference. The half-level arrays paph and geoh have NLEV+1 entries."
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "lev": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": "NLEV"
    },
    "lev_nodes": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": {
        "op": "+",
        "args": [
          "NLEV",
          1
        ]
      }
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "NTiedtkeColumnInitialization": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "./moist_thermodynamics.esm"
        }
      ],
      "subsystems": {
        "wrf": {
          "ref": "../../../lib/wrf_constants.esm"
        },
        "par": {
          "ref": "./ntiedtke_parameters.esm"
        }
      },
      "variables": {
        "T": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer temperature at the start of the convection step, top-down."
        },
        "q": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer water-vapour mixing ratio."
        },
        "qs": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer saturation mixing ratio, which `cu_ntiedtke_run` computes before calling `cumastrn` and passes straight through."
        },
        "u": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer zonal wind."
        },
        "v": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer meridional wind."
        },
        "omega": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer vertical velocity in pressure coordinates; NEGATIVE is ascent, which is why the level of strongest ascent is a minimum."
        },
        "geo": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Layer geopotential."
        },
        "paph": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level pressure, increasing with the index because the ordering is top-down."
        },
        "geoh": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev_nodes"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level geopotential."
        },
        "ph": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level pressure at the interface that bounds layer k from above, i.e. `paph` read over the LAYER axis. The scheme's half-level fields are layer-shaped and indexed by the layer they sit above, so this gather is the bridge between the NLEV+1 interface array WRF supplies and the NLEV-long half-level fields cuinin builds."
        },
        "geoh_lev": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level geopotential read over the layer axis, the counterpart of `ph`."
        },
        "s_layer": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Dry static energy of the layer, cp T + phi: the quantity conserved by a dry adiabatic displacement, and therefore the one the scheme interpolates instead of temperature."
        },
        "T_h0": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level temperature before the saturation adjustment: the LARGER of the two adjacent layers' dry static energies brought to the interface geopotential. The maximum is the first half of the static-stability adjustment. The top entry is the top layer's temperature."
        },
        "qs_h0": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level saturation mixing ratio before the adjustment: the upper layer's value. Its TOP entry is never assigned by the Fortran; it is set here to the top layer's value and masked out of the tests."
        },
        "adjusted": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "1 at the half levels where `cuinin` actually calls the moist adjustment, 2 <= k <= NLEV-2. The Fortran's `cycle` skips the call AND the humidity reconstruction that follows it at the two lowest half levels and at the top."
        },
        "T_h1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level temperature after the environmental moist adjustment. The adjustment moves the TEMPERATURE as well as the saturation value, because `cuadjtqn` updates both of its arguments -- easy to miss, since the call reads as if it were only computing q_sat at half levels."
        },
        "qs_h": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level saturation mixing ratio after the adjustment."
        },
        "q_h": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level water-vapour mixing ratio. Where the adjustment ran it is the upper layer's SUBSATURATION carried to the half level -- min(q, q_sat) of that layer plus however far the adjustment moved q_sat -- floored at zero; elsewhere it is the upper layer's mixing ratio, and at the two ends the adjacent layer's own."
        },
        "T_h2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Half-level temperature with the SURFACE entry replaced by the bottom layer's dry static energy alone, without the maximum -- there is no layer below it to take a maximum against."
        },
        "s_half": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Dry static energy at the half levels, before the stability sweep."
        },
        "s_stable": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m^2/s^2",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Running maximum of the half-level dry static energy from the SURFACE upward: max over j >= k of s_half[j]. This is the Fortran's downward sweep unrolled; it makes the profile non-decreasing upward, which is the definition of dry static stability."
        },
        "T_h": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "FINAL half-level temperature, from the stabilised dry static energy. The top and surface entries are left as they were, exactly as the Fortran's loop bounds leave them."
        },
        "wmin": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "Pa/s",
          "description": "Most negative omega in [3, NLEV], i.e. the strongest ascent in the column below the third level from the top."
        },
        "klwmin": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Level of strongest ascent, or the SURFACE level when nothing in [3, NLEV] is ascending. The Fortran finds it by a surface-to-top scan that updates only on a strict improvement, so among tied levels it keeps the one nearest the surface -- the LARGEST index in this top-down ordering."
        },
        "Tu": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught temperature, initialised to the half-level environment."
        },
        "Td": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "K",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught temperature, initialised to the same."
        },
        "qu": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught mixing ratio, initialised to the half-level environment."
        },
        "qd": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught mixing ratio, initialised to the same."
        },
        "lu": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught condensate, initialised to zero."
        },
        "uu": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught zonal wind, initialised to the layer ABOVE the half level (the upper layer), with the top half level taking the top layer."
        },
        "ud": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught zonal wind, initialised to the same."
        },
        "vu": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Updraught meridional wind, initialised to the upper layer's."
        },
        "vd": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "m/s",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Downdraught meridional wind, initialised to the same."
        },
        "lab": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "Cloud-layer label, initialised to zero everywhere; the trigger and the updraught set it to 1 below cloud base and 2 in the cloud."
        },
        "qs_h_defined": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "kg/kg",
          "shape": [
            "lev"
          ],
          "description": "`qs_h` with its TOP entry zeroed. `cuinin` never assigns `pqsenh(:,1)` although the array is `intent(out)`, so the Fortran's top entry is whatever memory held and there is nothing there to compare against; this is the field the tests assert, with the same entry zeroed on the reference side, so that every entry the Fortran actually defines is pinned and the one it does not is not pretended to match."
        }
      },
